  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nCurrent-Sunday...Very muggy conditions exist presently with temps \nand dewpoints well into the 70s. Surface high pressure ridging will \nslowly slip south into central FL during the day and further \nsoutheast & seaward on Sun. Light SW/W winds will transition onshore \nin the afternoon along the coast with ECSB formation and slow push \ninland (quicker across the Treasure Coast). A late day/early evening \nsea breeze collision both days this weekend will occur across the \neastern peninsula - further toward the east coast than on Fri. Storm \nsteering is light WRLY which will bring activity back toward the \ncoast and across the intracoastal and near shore Atlc waters. Weak \ntroughing in the mid-levels will exist along the FL east coast today \nwith some mid-level energy available for aiding convection, though \ntemps at 500 mb remain fairly warm at -5.5C to -6.5C, slightly \ncooler than on Fri. Similar temps aloft on Sun, though the \naforementioned mid-level troughing will be mainly southeast of the \narea. PWATs remain near to slightly above 2\", esp Cape southward \ntoday and perhaps some drier air pushing across the area on Sun. \nMainly diurnal PoPs 50-70% today and 50-60% for Sun. Primary storm \nimpacts remain frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds locally 35-45 \nmph, and torrential downpours capable of 2-4\" in a relatively short \nperiod promoting minor/nuisance flooding in slow-moving storms or \nrepeated rounds. The Weather Prediction Center maintains a Marginal \nRisk for excessive rainfall for portions of east central FL thru the \nweekend. \n\nMax temps near to slightly above climo near 90F to L90s. Humid \novernight with mins in the 70s. Peak heat indices continue 100-107F \nahead of daily storms.\n\nMon-Fri...Troughiness in the mid-levels moves slowly away from the \neastern seaboard and further into the western Atlc early in the \nperiod. Mid-level ridging across the Plains will strengthen and \nbecome more expansive toward the Deep South/southeast U.S. Weak east-\nwest oriented mid-level troughing will slowly slide down the \npeninsula becoming fixated across the central FL peninsula into mid-\nweek. This energy will aid diurnal convection thru the period. Mid-\nlevel temps fairly consistent -5.5C to -6.5C. At the surface, a weak \npressure pattern will remain in place. A weakening front will push \ninto the Deep South/north FL early next week finally washing out by \naround mid-week. The NBM continues to favor higher than normal PoPs \nthru mid next week. Storm impacts continue to revolve around locally \nheavy rainfall. The ECSB will develop each afternoon, but will \nremain limited across the eastern FL peninsula into Mon, but much \ndepends on the southward progression of the aforementioned weak \nfrontal boundary to the north. Storm motion still light offshore \nthru Tue, then more light/variable into the rest of the extended. \nGenerally near seasonal temps for highs (U80s-L90s) with lows in the \n70s continuing.",
